1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,707
This is MATH 239, video 1. The topic is the basic counting rules: AND and OR, lists, permutations, and subsets.

2
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:15,618
After this video, you can turn AND into multiplication, and OR into addition.

3
00:00:16,068 --> 00:00:20,911
You can count lists, subsets, partial lists, and subsets of a given size.

4
00:00:21,361 --> 00:00:26,438
You can prove the Pascal identity by counting, and build Pascal's triangle.

5
00:00:26,888 --> 00:00:34,952
And you can solve practice problems P1-1, P1-2, and P1-7 parts a and b.

6
00:00:35,402 --> 00:00:46,666
We count objects that we make by a sequence of choices. In Example 1.1 of the notes, a table has 7 apples, 8 oranges, and 5 bananas.

7
00:00:47,116 --> 00:00:51,361
The question: how many ways are there to choose an apple AND a banana?

8
00:00:51,811 --> 00:00:59,619
Take the first apple, A 1. It goes with each of the 5 bananas. That gives the 5 pairs in the orange row.

9
00:01:00,069 --> 00:01:07,685
Each of the 7 apples gives its own row of 5 pairs. So the grid has 7 rows, one for each apple.

10
00:01:08,135 --> 00:01:11,421
The grid has 5 columns, one for each banana.

11
00:01:11,871 --> 00:01:18,804
So there are 7 times 5 choices, which is 35. The word AND tells us to multiply.

12
00:01:19,254 --> 00:01:21,601
Now choose an apple OR an orange.

13
00:01:22,051 --> 00:01:23,693
There are 7 apples.

14
00:01:24,143 --> 00:01:25,871
There are 8 oranges.

15
00:01:26,321 --> 00:01:33,191
Put all the possible choices in one row. The 7 apples come first, and then the 8 oranges.

16
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:38,931
No fruit is both an apple and an orange. So the two groups do not overlap.

17
00:01:39,381 --> 00:01:44,928
So there are 7 plus 8 choices, which is 15. The word OR tells us to add.

18
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:49,175
Next: choose an apple, AND either an orange or a banana.

19
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:59,417
For each apple, the second fruit is one of 8 oranges OR one of 5 bananas. So each row has 8 plus 5, which is 13 cells.

20
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:02,875
There are 7 rows, one for each apple.

21
00:02:03,325 --> 00:02:07,592
So there are 7 times 13 choices, which is 91.

22
00:02:08,042 --> 00:02:12,991
Now a different sentence: choose either an apple AND an orange, OR a banana.

23
00:02:13,441 --> 00:02:20,908
The first option is a pair of an apple and an orange. The pairs form a grid with 7 rows and 8 columns.

24
00:02:21,358 --> 00:02:25,262
The second option is one banana. There are 5 bananas.

25
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:31,365
OR means add. 7 times 8 is 56. Plus 5, that is 61.

26
00:02:31,815 --> 00:02:37,938
The same three numbers give 91 or 61. The difference is where the brackets go.

27
00:02:38,388 --> 00:02:42,740
So read the description carefully, and find how AND and OR combine.

28
00:02:43,190 --> 00:02:46,646
In the language of sets, AND is the Cartesian product.

29
00:02:47,096 --> 00:02:52,621
A cross B is the set of all ordered pairs a, b, with a in A and b in B.

30
00:02:53,071 --> 00:02:56,485
Its size is the size of A times the size of B.

31
00:02:56,935 --> 00:03:02,140
OR is the union. A union B has the elements that are in A or in B.

32
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:07,177
The intersection, A intersect B, has the elements that are in both.

33
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:13,941
Here is an added example. A is the set of even numbers up to 10. It has 5 elements.

34
00:03:14,391 --> 00:03:19,106
B is the set of multiples of 3 up to 10. It has 3 elements.

35
00:03:19,556 --> 00:03:24,207
5 plus 3 is 8. But the union has fewer than 8 elements.

36
00:03:24,657 --> 00:03:29,585
The number 6 is in both sets. So 5 plus 3 counts the 6 twice.

37
00:03:30,035 --> 00:03:37,629
Subtract the intersection one time. The size of A union B is 5 plus 3 minus 1, which is 7.

38
00:03:38,079 --> 00:03:51,733
In general, the size of the union is the size of A, plus the size of B, minus the size of the intersection. This is the first case of inclusion and exclusion, the topic of video 5.

39
00:03:52,183 --> 00:04:00,823
If A and B have no common element, the union is a disjoint union. Then the size of the union is the sum of the sizes.

40
00:04:01,273 --> 00:04:06,073
So before you turn OR into addition, check that the two sets are disjoint.

41
00:04:06,523 --> 00:04:11,984
A list of a set S contains each element of S exactly once, in some order.

42
00:04:12,434 --> 00:04:17,106
The notes list the set S with the four elements 1, a, X, and g.

43
00:04:17,556 --> 00:04:22,292
Start with the lists that begin with 1. Their first entries are orange.

44
00:04:22,742 --> 00:04:29,249
After the 1 comes a list of the other three elements: a, X, and g. There are 6 such lists.

45
00:04:29,699 --> 00:04:34,072
Each of the other columns begins with a, with X, or with g.

46
00:04:34,522 --> 00:04:39,877
So to make a list, choose the first entry v, AND then a list of S without v.

47
00:04:40,327 --> 00:04:54,236
Let p n be the number of lists of an n element set. There are n choices for the first entry, AND p of n minus 1 lists of the rest. So p n equals n times p of n minus 1.

48
00:04:54,686 --> 00:05:03,859
The table has 4 columns of 6 lists. So p 4 equals 4 times p 3, which is 4 times 6, or 24.

49
00:05:04,309 --> 00:05:11,456
Here is the same idea as a choice tree, for the set 1, 2, 3. The first entry has 3 choices.

50
00:05:11,906 --> 00:05:17,602
For each first entry, the second entry has 2 choices: the two elements that are left.

51
00:05:18,052 --> 00:05:20,804
Then 1 choice remains for the last entry.

52
00:05:21,254 --> 00:05:27,675
Each path from the top to the bottom gives one list. The orange path gives the list 1, 2, 3.

53
00:05:28,125 --> 00:05:31,539
There are 3 times 2 times 1, which is 6 lists.

54
00:05:31,989 --> 00:05:41,269
Theorem 1.2: for n at least 1, an n element set has n times n minus 1, and so on down to 1, lists.

55
00:05:41,719 --> 00:05:46,135
This product is n factorial, written n with an exclamation mark.

56
00:05:46,585 --> 00:05:53,689
A permutation is a list of the set 1 to n. So there are n factorial permutations of size n.

57
00:05:54,139 --> 00:06:00,347
The proof is by induction on n. It uses p n equals n times p of n minus 1.

58
00:06:00,797 --> 00:06:04,786
Zero factorial is the number of lists of the empty set.

59
00:06:05,236 --> 00:06:15,988
We want p 1 equals 1 times p 0 to be true. Since p 1 equals 1, p 0 must be 1. So zero factorial equals 1.

60
00:06:16,438 --> 00:06:19,019
Take the permutation 3 1 4 2.

61
00:06:19,469 --> 00:06:21,709
Position 1 has the entry 3.

62
00:06:22,159 --> 00:06:24,954
Position 2 has the entry 1, and so on.

63
00:06:25,404 --> 00:06:34,257
We can read a permutation as a function sigma from the set 1 to n to itself. Sigma of i is the entry in position i.

64
00:06:34,707 --> 00:06:38,291
So sigma of 1 is 3, and sigma of 2 is 1.

65
00:06:38,741 --> 00:06:42,240
Sigma of 3 is 4, and sigma of 4 is 2.

66
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:53,485
Each number on the right gets exactly one arrow, because a list contains each element exactly once. Video 3 uses this idea for bijections.

67
00:06:53,935 --> 00:06:58,927
A subset of S contains some of the elements of S, perhaps none or all.

68
00:06:59,377 --> 00:07:03,857
Each element is in it at most once, and the order does not matter.

69
00:07:04,307 --> 00:07:16,787
To choose a subset A of the set a, b, c, decide for each element: in A, or not in A. For the element a, that is 2 choices. Green means in A, and gray means not in A.

70
00:07:17,237 --> 00:07:21,205
AND for the element b, there are again 2 choices: in or out.

71
00:07:21,655 --> 00:07:23,809
AND there are 2 choices for c.

72
00:07:24,259 --> 00:07:31,619
Each path from the top to the bottom gives one subset. There are 2 times 2 times 2, which is 8 subsets.

73
00:07:32,069 --> 00:07:36,379
The last path says no to each element. It gives the empty set.

74
00:07:36,829 --> 00:07:43,250
Theorem 1.3: for n at least 0, an n element set has 2 to the n subsets.

75
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:49,161
The proof: there are 2 choices for each of the n elements, and AND means multiply.

76
00:07:49,611 --> 00:07:56,225
A partial list of S is a list of a subset of S. The entries are different, and the order matters.

77
00:07:56,675 --> 00:08:01,581
The notes count the partial lists x y z of length 3 of the set a to f.

78
00:08:02,031 --> 00:08:06,725
The first entry x has 6 choices: a to f. Here we choose c.

79
00:08:07,175 --> 00:08:15,303
The second entry y must be different from x. So y has 5 choices, because c is crossed out. Here we choose a.

80
00:08:15,753 --> 00:08:22,601
The third entry z must be different from x and y. So z has 4 choices. Here we choose f.

81
00:08:23,051 --> 00:08:25,632
So this partial list is c, a, f.

82
00:08:26,082 --> 00:08:32,354
There are 6 times 5 times 4 partial lists of length 3. That is 120.

83
00:08:32,804 --> 00:08:46,180
Theorem 1.4: an n element set has n times n minus 1, and so on down to n minus k plus 1, partial lists of length k. The product has k factors.

84
00:08:46,630 --> 00:08:54,246
If k is larger than n, one factor is n minus n, which is 0. Then there are no such partial lists.

85
00:08:54,696 --> 00:09:00,819
If k is at most n, the product equals n factorial over n minus k factorial.

86
00:09:01,269 --> 00:09:10,015
Now count the subsets with exactly k elements. Let n choose k be the number of k element subsets of an n element set.

87
00:09:10,465 --> 00:09:20,257
Start with an example: n equals 4 and k equals 3. Here are all the partial lists of length 3 of the set a, b, c, d.

88
00:09:20,707 --> 00:09:26,595
By Theorem 1.4 there are 4 times 3 times 2 of them, which is 24.

89
00:09:27,045 --> 00:09:35,643
Group the partial lists by their set of entries. Each purple group is one 3 element subset, for example a, b, c.

90
00:09:36,093 --> 00:09:43,581
Each group contains all the lists of its subset. A 3 element set has 3 factorial lists, which is 6.

91
00:09:44,031 --> 00:09:51,839
Take the partial list b, c, a. It is a choice of a 3 element subset, a, b, c, AND a list of that subset.

92
00:09:52,289 --> 00:10:02,571
So 4 choose 3 times 6 equals 24. So 4 choose 3 is 24 over 6, which is 4. These are the 4 purple groups.

93
00:10:03,021 --> 00:10:13,539
The same argument works for all n and k. Choose a k element subset, AND a list of it. Each partial list of length k is made exactly once.

94
00:10:13,989 --> 00:10:21,050
So n choose k times k factorial equals n factorial over n minus k factorial.

95
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:29,607
Divide by k factorial. This gives Theorem 1.5. We call n choose k a binomial coefficient.

96
00:10:30,057 --> 00:10:37,843
Section 1.1.3 gives a strategy. Do not start with algebra. First ask what each number counts.

97
00:10:38,293 --> 00:10:46,784
Example 1.6 is the identity: the sum of n choose k, for k from 0 to n, equals 2 to the n.

98
00:10:47,234 --> 00:10:53,655
Take n equals 3. Here are the 8 subsets of the set a, b, c. 2 to the 3 counts them.

99
00:10:54,105 --> 00:10:56,324
Sort the subsets by their size k.

100
00:10:56,774 --> 00:11:04,667
The column for size k has n choose k subsets. Size 0 has 3 choose 0, which is 1 subset.

101
00:11:05,117 --> 00:11:08,701
Size 1 has 3 choose 1, which is 3 subsets.

102
00:11:09,151 --> 00:11:12,714
Size 2 has 3 choose 2, which is 3 subsets.

103
00:11:13,164 --> 00:11:16,769
Size 3 has 3 choose 3, which is 1 subset.

104
00:11:17,219 --> 00:11:22,318
So 1 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1 equals 8, which is 2 to the 3.

105
00:11:22,768 --> 00:11:30,704
In general, each subset has exactly one size k. So the subsets of all sizes form a disjoint union.

106
00:11:31,154 --> 00:11:35,826
OR means add, so the identity is true. There is nothing more to prove.

107
00:11:36,276 --> 00:11:46,473
Example 1.7 is the identity: n choose k equals n minus 1 choose k minus 1, plus n minus 1 choose k.

108
00:11:46,923 --> 00:11:51,681
Here m choose j is 0 if j is negative or larger than m.

109
00:11:52,131 --> 00:11:55,779
The left side counts the k element subsets of 1 to n.

110
00:11:56,229 --> 00:12:00,751
The two terms on the right count subsets of 1 to n minus 1.

111
00:12:01,201 --> 00:12:08,284
Take n equals 5 and k equals 2. Here are the 10 subsets of size 2 of the set 1 to 5.

112
00:12:08,734 --> 00:12:12,681
Split them into two groups. The orange group contains 5.

113
00:12:13,131 --> 00:12:17,547
The blue group does not contain 5. The two groups are disjoint.

114
00:12:17,997 --> 00:12:24,717
Remove the 5 from each orange subset. What is left is a 1 element subset of 1 to 4.

115
00:12:25,167 --> 00:12:32,847
Each one comes from exactly one orange subset. So the orange group has 4 choose 1 subsets, which is 4.

116
00:12:33,297 --> 00:12:40,785
The blue subsets are exactly the 2 element subsets of 1 to 4. There are 4 choose 2 of them, which is 6.

117
00:12:41,235 --> 00:12:44,691
And 5 choose 2 is 10, which is 4 plus 6.

118
00:12:45,141 --> 00:12:51,029
Here is the proof for all n and k. Let A be a k element subset of 1 to n.

119
00:12:51,479 --> 00:12:58,625
If n is in A, then A without n is a k minus 1 element subset of 1 to n minus 1.

120
00:12:59,075 --> 00:13:04,707
If n is not in A, then A is a k element subset of 1 to n minus 1.

121
00:13:05,157 --> 00:13:09,765
Exactly one case is true. OR means add, so the identity is true.

122
00:13:10,215 --> 00:13:23,293
The Pascal identity gives a fast way to compute binomial coefficients. Put n choose k in row n, at position k. For example, row 4 is 1, 4, 6, 4, 1.

123
00:13:23,743 --> 00:13:30,036
The first and last entries of each row are n choose 0 and n choose n. Both equal 1.

124
00:13:30,486 --> 00:13:34,027
Each other entry is the sum of the two entries above it.

125
00:13:34,477 --> 00:13:41,752
For example, 5 choose 2 is 4 choose 1 plus 4 choose 2. That is 4 plus 6, which is 10.

126
00:13:42,202 --> 00:13:46,597
Continue row by row. The notes show the table up to row 8.

127
00:13:47,047 --> 00:13:53,361
Row 8 is 1, 8, 28, 56, 70, 56, 28, 8, 1.

128
00:13:53,811 --> 00:14:01,662
Each row reads the same from both ends. For example, 8 choose 2 and 8 choose 6 both equal 28.

129
00:14:02,112 --> 00:14:09,088
The formula of Theorem 1.5 shows why: n choose k equals n choose n minus k.

130
00:14:09,538 --> 00:14:15,106
Now the practice problems of Assignment 1. Let S be the set 1 to 100.

131
00:14:15,556 --> 00:14:19,375
Part a: how many subsets of S do not contain 37?

132
00:14:19,825 --> 00:14:27,014
Part b: how many subsets contain 13 and 42, but not 37? Pause the video and try it.

133
00:14:27,464 --> 00:14:35,336
Part a. For each element, we decide: in or out. For 37 the decision is fixed: out. That is 1 choice.

134
00:14:35,786 --> 00:14:41,781
Each of the other 100 minus 1, which is 99, elements has 2 choices.

135
00:14:42,231 --> 00:14:45,239
So there are 2 to the 99 such subsets.

136
00:14:45,689 --> 00:15:01,241
A full answer says why. A subset of S without 37 is a subset of S without the element 37. That set has 99 elements. By Theorem 1.3, there are 2 to the 99 subsets.

137
00:15:01,691 --> 00:15:10,032
Part b. Now 13 and 42 must be in, and 37 must be out. These three elements have 1 choice each.

138
00:15:10,482 --> 00:15:15,133
The other 100 minus 3, which is 97, elements are free.

139
00:15:15,583 --> 00:15:18,420
So there are 2 to the 97 subsets.

140
00:15:18,870 --> 00:15:27,233
A full answer: each such subset is 13 and 42, together with a subset T of the other 97 elements.

141
00:15:27,683 --> 00:15:36,621
Each T gives one subset, and each subset gives one T. By Theorem 1.3, the answer is 2 to the 97.

142
00:15:37,071 --> 00:15:48,079
Practice problem P1-2. A poker hand is 5 cards from a deck of 52 different cards. How many hands are there? Pause the video and try it.

143
00:15:48,529 --> 00:15:52,668
Here is the deck as a grid. There are 4 suits, one in each row.

144
00:15:53,118 --> 00:15:56,105
There are 13 ranks, one in each column.

145
00:15:56,555 --> 00:15:59,541
4 times 13 is 52 cards.

146
00:15:59,991 --> 00:16:03,575
One hand is shown in orange. It has 5 cards.

147
00:16:04,025 --> 00:16:13,199
The order of the cards in a hand does not matter. A card cannot be in a hand twice. So a hand is a 5 element subset of the deck.

148
00:16:13,649 --> 00:16:28,411
By Theorem 1.5, the number of hands is 52 choose 5. This is 52 factorial, over 5 factorial times 47 factorial. Here 47 is 52 minus 5.

149
00:16:28,861 --> 00:16:40,552
52 factorial over 47 factorial leaves the five factors 52, 51, 50, 49, and 48. They are in the purple cells.

150
00:16:41,002 --> 00:16:52,181
Their product is 311,875,200. By Theorem 1.4, this is the number of ordered hands.

151
00:16:52,631 --> 00:17:09,676
5 factorial is 120. So 52 choose 5 is 311,875,200 over 120, which is 2,598,960.

152
00:17:10,126 --> 00:17:16,889
Practice problem P1-7. A is the set a, b, c. B is the set 1 to 5.

153
00:17:17,339 --> 00:17:22,011
Part a: how many functions from A to B are there? Pause the video and try it.

154
00:17:22,461 --> 00:17:26,770
A function gives each element of A exactly one value in B.

155
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:33,172
Choose the value of a. There are 5 choices. The dashed lines show them. Here we choose 2.

156
00:17:33,622 --> 00:17:41,771
Choose the value of b. Again there are 5 choices, because two elements may have the same value. Here we choose 5.

157
00:17:42,221 --> 00:17:46,979
Choose the value of c. There are 5 choices. Here we choose 2 again.

158
00:17:47,429 --> 00:17:51,525
So f of a is 2, f of b is 5, and f of c is 2.

159
00:17:51,975 --> 00:18:04,476
A function is a choice for a, AND a choice for b, AND a choice for c. So there are 5 times 5 times 5, which is 5 to the 3, or 125 functions.

160
00:18:04,926 --> 00:18:10,729
Part b: how many injective functions from A to B are there? Pause the video and try it.

161
00:18:11,179 --> 00:18:18,923
A function is injective if different elements of A have different values. Video 3 gives the full definition.

162
00:18:19,373 --> 00:18:22,935
The value of a has 5 choices. Here we choose 2.

163
00:18:23,385 --> 00:18:29,508
The value of b must be different from 2. So b has 4 choices. Here we choose 5.

164
00:18:29,958 --> 00:18:36,721
The value of c must be different from 2 and 5. So c has 3 choices. Here we choose 1.

165
00:18:37,171 --> 00:18:42,760
In total there are 5 times 4 times 3, which is 60 injective functions.

166
00:18:43,210 --> 00:18:53,791
Another view: the values f of a, f of b, f of c are three different elements of B, in order. That is a partial list of length 3 of B.

167
00:18:54,241 --> 00:19:03,820
So Theorem 1.4 gives 5 times 4 times 3 directly. Part c, about surjective functions, is in video 5.

168
00:19:04,270 --> 00:19:11,950
To recap. A list of an n element set: the order matters, and there are no repeats. There are n factorial lists.

169
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:17,051
A subset: no order and no repeats. There are 2 to the n subsets.

170
00:19:17,501 --> 00:19:23,197
A partial list of length k: there are n factorial over n minus k factorial.

171
00:19:23,647 --> 00:19:26,975
A k element subset: there are n choose k.

172
00:19:27,425 --> 00:19:35,339
A function from A to B allows repeated values. There are the size of B to the power of the size of A functions.

173
00:19:35,789 --> 00:19:40,547
AND means multiply, and OR means add, but only for a disjoint union.

174
00:19:40,997 --> 00:19:44,410
To prove an identity, think of what each number counts.

175
00:19:44,860 --> 00:19:50,364
The next video counts multisets, where repeats are allowed but the order does not matter.
